source.tar.tmp
target
wget-log
.eos-bl-src/
.eos-uu-src/
.eos-orbdata-src/
*.key
/book
# Dropped into the repository ROOT by `mdbook-mermaid install .`, which both the `pages` job and a
# local `mdbook build` run. They are generated assets of the book, not sources: measured 2026-09-03,
# building the book locally left them untracked in `git status`, which is how a generated file ends
# up committed by someone tidying their working tree (§5.7 forbids hand-editing generated files —
# committing them is the same mistake one step earlier).
/mermaid.min.js
/mermaid-init.js
/release/*.img
/release/*.tar.gz
# Release checksums + signature are build outputs regenerated per-release by
# scripts/make-release.sh and shipped as release ASSETS — never a committed
# trust anchor (a committed copy goes stale and references non-shipped images).
/release/SHA256SUMS
/release/*.minisig
.eos-kernel-src
.eos-drivers-src
.eos-base-src
.DS_Store
# macOS AppleDouble sidecars (._foo) — appear when the tree is copied through
# macOS Finder onto exFAT/SMB; pure metadata noise on every other OS.
._*

# ── repo-signing SECRET keys — never tracked, never staged ──────────────────────────
# There was no entry here at all, so `keys/eos-repo-sign.secret.toml` would have been
# untracked but NOT ignored: a plain `git add -A` would have staged the release signing key.
# Ignoring it does not make committing it impossible (`git add -f` still works, and check 10
# in ci-integrity.sh catches the material itself), but it removes the accident.
keys/*.secret.toml
keys/*secret*
keys/*.key
!keys/*.pub
!keys/*.pub.toml
# ...but never the macOS AppleDouble shadows. The two negations above re-admit anything
# matching keys/*.pub.toml, and `._eos-repo-sign.pub.toml` matches it -- so the `._*` rule
# further up was overridden and one such file got committed (U-196). A negation has to be
# narrowed again straight afterwards, or it lets junk back in through the door it opened.
keys/._*

# Secure Boot signing key placed for the bootloader recipe (V2-N03) — off-repo, never tracked
build/sb-signing/
build/boot-signing/

# ── OS and editor cruft ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea/
.vscode/
*.sublime-*

# ── Secrets — belt and braces on top of gitleaks and ci-integrity.sh ────────
# Nothing matching these has ever been committed (gitleaks over 8153 commits,
# one hit and it was a public key). These patterns exist so a slip is caught by
# git itself, before any scanner has to notice.
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.key
!keys/*.pub
!keys/**/*.pub
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.toml
id_rsa
*.secret.toml
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.kdbx
credentials.json

# Python bytecode caches. Added 2026-09-01 after two .pyc files reached `main` in one day,
# both through `git add -A` in commits of mine, and neither caught by any gate. CLAUDE.md
# already forbade committing caches; nothing enforced it.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Guard AFTER every negation above (CL-09, 2026-09-03): `!keys/**/*.pub` at line ~95 re-admitted
# `keys/._eos-release.pub` -- measured with `git check-ignore -v --no-index`. A negation must be
# narrowed again straight afterwards, or it lets the junk back in.
keys/**/._*
**/._*
**/.DS_Store
